notes:
Found live while verifying mayor-2mtvs (CR orphan-delete cascade-order fix): creating a namespaced CR (via create_cr_namespaced, cr.rs) with metadata.ownerReferences in the request body silently drops them — the stored object has no ownerReferences at all, even though the identical request against the cluster-scoped create_cr path (no such bug) preserves them correctly (there is an existing regression test create_cr_via_api_preserves_owner_references for the cluster-scoped path only).

Root cause: create_cr_namespaced does an EXTRA ObjectMeta round-trip (cr.rs, right before stamp_cr_fields is called) solely to set metadata.namespace:
    let mut meta: crate::types::ObjectMeta = serde_json::from_value(obj["metadata"].take()).unwrap_or_default();
    meta.namespace = Some(ns.clone());
    obj["metadata"] = serde_json::to_value(meta).unwrap_or_default();
    stamp_cr_fields(&mut obj, &group, &version, &ctx.kind);
ObjectMeta's serde impl only knows declared fields (ownerReferences is a fixed field it DOES declare, actually — need to double-check whether ObjectMeta itself has an ownerReferences field or not; live repro showed it disappears, so either ObjectMeta lacks the field or something else strips it). stamp_cr_fields has an explicit save-before/restore-after specifically because IT does this same round-trip and learned the hard way (see its comment) — but the namespace-setting round-trip above it does not, so by the time stamp_cr_fields runs, ownerReferences is already gone and its own save is a no-op.

Impact: any namespaced CR created with an explicit ownerReference (kubectl create/apply of a CR-owns-CR dependent, or any controller doing so) silently loses the owner link. Breaks GC/owner-based cascade for ALL namespaced CRs (cluster-scoped CRs are unaffected). Live-reproduced via kubectl create (not just apply) against a namespaced CRD instance with ownerReferences in this session's Lima VM run.

Fix direction: apply the same save-owner-refs-before / restore-after pattern that stamp_cr_fields already uses, to the namespace-setting ObjectMeta round-trip in create_cr_namespaced — or reorder so stamp_cr_fields (which already does the safe round-trip) is the ONLY place ObjectMeta gets round-tripped, and fold the namespace assignment into it or do it via a direct JSON field set (obj["metadata"]["namespace"] = ...) instead of an ObjectMeta round-trip.

Needs its own regression test mirroring create_cr_via_api_preserves_owner_references but calling create_cr_namespaced instead of create_cr.

description:
Found by the schema-mapping-completeness audit (2026-07-15), flagged for urgent triage rather than fixed inline (audit was read-only).

crates/apiserver/src/apps_gen_adapter.rs:15-26's gen_label_selector_to_json ONLY handles matchLabels -- it has no matchExpressions branch at all:

fn gen_label_selector_to_json(sel: meta_v1::LabelSelector) -> serde_json::Value {
    let mut m = serde_json::json!({});
    if !sel.match_labels.is_empty() {
        ... m["matchLabels"] = ...
    }
    m   // matchExpressions is silently dropped, always
}

This function is reached by gen_apps_spec_to_json (apps_gen_adapter.rs:48-57), called from all four of decode_statefulset_proto_gen (:79), decode_deployment_proto_gen (:169), decode_daemonset_proto_gen (:263), decode_replicaset_proto_gen (:325).

IMPACT: any Deployment/ReplicaSet/DaemonSet/StatefulSet whose spec.selector uses matchExpressions (not just matchLabels), decoded via the protobuf wire path (the DEFAULT content-type for client-go typed clients -- kube-controller-manager itself, e2e test binaries), silently loses matchExpressions on decode. Worst case: if matchLabels is empty and only matchExpressions was set, the decoded selector becomes {} -- and upstream Kubernetes semantics (metav1.LabelSelectorAsSelector) treat an empty/absent LabelSelector as "select everything," so the controller could adopt/count/act on every pod in the namespace instead of the intended subset.

RELATED (fix alongside, same function/area): admissionreg_gen_adapter.rs:92-117's gen_label_selector_to_json has already textually diverged from the canonical core_gen_adapter.rs:991-1013 version -- canonical omits empty key/operator/values per-matchExpressions-entry, the admissionreg copy emits them unconditionally (unwrap_or_default()). Cosmetic today but same drift risk; fix to match canonical while touching this area.

Audit findings doc (if still available): ai/findings/schema-mapping-completeness-audit-2026-07-15.md (was worker-local/gitignored, may no longer exist -- this description is self-contained).

Not yet reproduced at runtime (audit was read-only, static-analysis only) -- first step is a live kubectl repro confirming the worst-case selector-collapse before fixing.

DISCOVERED FROM mayor-qki6l while doing the mandatory LIVE verification for that bead's fix (DeleteCollection CR fallback + field-selector). After that fix, [sig-api-machinery] CustomResourceFieldSelectors progressed past every LIST, WATCH, and DELETE assertion (crd_selectable_fields.go:229-305 all pass now) but fails at the FINAL assertion:

  STEP: Waiting for v2 informer events after updates and deletes for field selector host=host1,port=80
  [FAILED] in [It] - k8s.io/kubernetes/test/e2e/apimachinery/crd_selectable_fields.go:309
  Expected added:{cr0} deleted:{cr0} but got added:{cr0} deleted:{cr0, cr1}

cr1 (host=host1,port=8080) never matched this informer's fieldSelector "host=host1,port=80" (only cr0, host=host1/port=80, matched — confirmed by the "added" set containing only cr0). cr1 is later updated (host changed to "host2", port unchanged) via a plain Update call — it still does not match the filter after the update. Yet the informer receives a spurious DELETED event for cr1, which it was never told about via ADDED in the first place.

ROOT CAUSE: crates/apiserver/src/handlers/watch.rs, the MODIFIED-event handling in watch_generic_impl's chunk_stream (~line 752-846). When a live WatchEvent::Modified arrives and the (converted) body does NOT match the label/field selector, the code takes this branch:

  } else if is_modified && !locally_deleted.contains(&obj.key) {
      // emit synthetic DELETED
      ...
  }

This branch only checks (a) the object doesn't match NOW and (b) a synthetic DELETE hasn't already been sent for this key. It does NOT check whether the object EVER matched the selector before this MODIFIED event (i.e., whether this specific watcher was ever told ADDED for it). For an object that never matched to begin with, any subsequent MODIFIED event (even one that still doesn't match) incorrectly emits a synthetic DELETED — a phantom delete for an object the watcher's cache never contained.

This is NOT specific to CR field selectors — the same `field_selector_matches`/`object_matches_label_selector` gate feeds both the built-in label-selector watch path and the CR field-selector path (cr_matches_field_selector), so a label-selector-filtered watch could hit the identical bug given the same shape of events (an always-non-matching object receiving a MODIFIED). It was not previously caught because the existing regression test (mayor-dymy, watch.rs ~line 2061, 'MODIFIED event that removes matching label must emit a synthetic DELETED') only covers the case where the object matched BEFORE the modification, not the case where it never matched.

FIX SHAPE (not yet implemented — needs design): track, per watcher, whether each key has ever been emitted as ADDED/MODIFIED (not just whether a synthetic-or-real DELETE was already sent) so the synthetic-DELETE branch can be gated on 'was this object ever in this watcher's scope', not just 'does it not match now'. `locally_deleted` alone (a set of keys a DELETE was already sent for) is insufficient — need something like the inverse: a set/knowledge of keys currently believed present in the watcher's scope.

VERIFY: reproduced live via scripts/conformance/run-all.sh --vm lima-node-3 --port 6445 --kubelet-port 10252 --focus "CustomResourceFieldSelectors" — e2e.txt shows the exact failure above (line 309, extra unexpected delete event, run 0721-2023-customresourcefieldselectors). A minimal repro: open a label-selector (or CR field-selector) watch, create/observe an object that never matches, then Update it (still non-matching) — expect zero DELETED events for it; today one fires.

## Goal

Separate "API object representation" (serde_json::Value) from "wire format" (JSON bytes vs protobuf bytes) behind a trait. This makes handler unit tests format-agnostic, isolates the proto re-encoding path, and makes it easy to add new serializers (e.g. full typed proto) without touching handlers.

## Design

```rust
// crates/apiserver/src/serializer.rs (new file)
pub(crate) trait ApiSerializer: Send + Sync {
    fn serialize(&self, val: &serde_json::Value) -> bytes::Bytes;
    fn content_type(&self) -> &'static str;
}

pub(crate) struct JsonSerializer;
impl ApiSerializer for JsonSerializer {
    fn serialize(&self, val: &serde_json::Value) -> bytes::Bytes {
        bytes::Bytes::from(val.to_string())
    }
    fn content_type(&self) -> &'static str { "application/json" }
}

pub(crate) struct ProtoSerializer;
impl ApiSerializer for ProtoSerializer {
    fn serialize(&self, val: &serde_json::Value) -> bytes::Bytes {
        crate::proto::encode_proto_response(val)
    }
    fn content_type(&self) -> &'static str {
        "application/vnd.kubernetes.protobuf"
    }
}
```

The content_type Tower middleware (`content_type.rs`) selects the serializer based on the Accept header and injects it (or a flag) into the response pipeline. Handlers themselves never inspect Accept — they always produce `serde_json::Value`, and the serializer layer handles wire format.

## Benefits

- Handler tests use `JsonSerializer` — no proto logic to worry about
- Proto path is fully isolated — easy to swap in typed proto per-kind later
- Adding a new format (e.g. CBOR, YAML) is one new impl, zero handler changes
- Cleaner than the current Tower middleware that intercepts and re-encodes after the fact

## Scope

- New file `crates/apiserver/src/serializer.rs` (~50 lines)
- Refactor `content_type.rs` to use the trait (~30 lines changed)
- No handler changes required if the middleware layer stays as-is
- Tests: JsonSerializer and ProtoSerializer round-trip tests

## Note

This is a prerequisite for mayor-fullproto (full typed proto support) but has standalone value for testability regardless.

